    "insert": "The motivation suffers from bad English and a bad writing style in general. For instance, the first sentence is not correct English. Further, the text reads more like a clickbait article rather than a scientific article. Also, the motivation that Gen AI can be used to support physicians to make more precise diagnoses and reduce misdiagnoses is not conveyed. Also, the paragraph does not provide clear evidence why Gen AI could help in this use case. Lastly, how is Gen AI going to be used in data analysis? Why Gen AI and not DL, …?\n\nThe Research Questions are way too broad and unfocused. Answering all of them is probably a PhD in itself.\n\n\nThe Approach chapter seems to be generated using an LLM. The style of writing and quality of spelling are too different from the first section. As such, I also observed the typical issues with generated text in this section. Namely, imprecision, wordiness, mixed contexts, weird claims, and strange conclusions.\nFor instance, “Gen AI […] allowing medical professionals and researchers to focus on more complex and high-value activities”. How is this relevant in this context? Why is this sentence needed? What is the point being made here? Then the paragraph drifts into synthetic data generation, which is not at all part of the work. Also, no connection is made to the own work. The text shortly mentions ML and goes right back to Gen AI without mentioning why Gen AI is the way to go for the work. Further, the work wants to find “six physicians who actively work with generative AI models in hospitals”. Good luck with finding them, as I am quite sure there are zero. Another example: To mitigate the effect of over-reliance on AI output, the author proposes cross-validation. How is this helping? How does this even work?"
